ke for better predictions.\n# Usefulness: Credit amounts often have a skewed distribution. Using log transformation will improve the model\'s ability to capture patterns from extreme values (e.g., very high credit amounts). This can help stabilize model predictions and reduce the influence of outliers.\ndf[\'log_credit_amount\'] = df[\'credit_amount\'].apply(lambda x: np.log(x + 1))\n```end\n\n---\n\n2. **Step 2: Age Binning**\n\n```python\n# Feature name: age_bin\n# Feature description: Bins the age feature into discrete intervals (e.g., young adult, middle-aged, senior) to capture potential non-linear trends in credit risk across age groups.\n# Usefulness: Age might relate to creditworthiness non-linearly. Binning can introduce domain-specific knowledge by grouping individuals into meaningful age ranges, helping the model discern credit behavior patterns for these groups.\nbins = [18, 30, 45, 60, 100]  # Defining meaningful bins\nlabels = [\'young\', \'middle-aged\', \'senior\', \'elder\']\ndf[\'age_bin\'] = pd.cut(df[\'age\'], bins=bins, labels=labels, right=False)\n```end\n\n---\n\n3. **Step 3: Interaction Between Employment and Savings Status**\n\n```python\n# Feature name: employment_savings_interaction\n# Feature description: Creates an interaction feature by combining employment status and savings_status, capturing relationships between job stability and savings behavior, which can improve credit risk prediction.\n# Usefulness: Combining these categorical variables can capture nuances in how job stability interacts with savings status, e.g., someone unemployed with no savings might pose a higher risk than someone employed with substantial savings.\ndf[\'employment_savings_interaction\'] = df[\'employment\'].astype(str) + "_" + df[\'savings_status\'].astype(str)\ndf = pd.get_dummies(df, columns=[\'employment_savings_interaction\'])\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns:\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column checking_status_<0 is dropped\n# checking_stat