re description: Combine \'property_magnitude\' and \'savings_status\' into a cross-tabulated categorical feature that describes their interaction.\n# Usefulness: Combining \'property_magnitude\' and \'savings_status\' may capture latent relationships where individuals with higher savings and property ownership might indicate lower credit risk. Models can better identify patterns in combined variables that remain obscure when studied individually.\ndf[\'interaction_property_savings_status\'] = df[\'property_magnitude\'] + "_" + df[\'savings_status\']\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Dropping columns:\n```python\n# Explanation why the column \'checking_status_<0\' is dropped\n# The column \'checking_status_<0\' is redundant due to the existence of other binary columns derived from `checking_status` \n# (e.g., \'checking_status_no checking\' and \'checking_status_>=200\'), making it unnecessary for the model.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'checking_status_<0\'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column \'own_telephone\' is dropped\n# The column \'own_telephone\' has limited variability (categories: \'yes\', \'none\'). This likely adds minimal predictive value\n# since other features such as \'job\' and \'income-related features\' are already stronger indicators of financial behavior.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'own_telephone\'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column \'personal_status\' is dropped\n# The column \'personal_status\' encodes a mix of marital status and sex in a single variable, making it harder to interpret and use effectively in models.\n# Extracting sex and marital status separately, or dropping it for simplicity, might be a better approach here.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'personal_status\'], inplace=True)\n```end']
